end 'greenMain'

// runTurn runs on a behaviour's green thread, after the behaviour, the behaviours that the releases hand on to it (handOn),
// one after another, so the messages queued on a busy service run on one green thread with no switch between them. A
// turn is turnBehaviours behaviours at most, as maxon's runner takes 64 messages a turn, and ends sooner when the monitor
// has asked the green thread to stop, which it could not do in the runtime's code between two behaviours, or when a
// behaviour made a coroutine, whose strand the green thread now roots and which the next behaviour is no member of: the
// behaviour handed on then goes to the tail of the processor's run queue, behind what waited meanwhile, as maxon's
// runner yields after its 64, and the green thread finishes. A behaviour of the turn may be preempted, or park, as the
// first may; the green thread holds only the cowns of the behaviour it runs, and the thread it runs on is read again after
